# tally 2.1.8 R11 (pick P26, item 1 of the follow-up; g14/past_64_counts g1_none): add_business past the day range
# meets the calendar's refusal first in day order, and that refusal stands (g_none forwards on cn, which has no
# business day; g_back backwards into cq's 2025, which has none); a hole met first is outranked by the edge's
# RUN-LIMIT (g_hole: cr's 2027). 2.1.8's first refcore refused RUN-LIMIT before reading the calendar.
=== m.tally
model a6
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source plan "plan"
input d: date
input cr: calendar
input cq: calendar
input cn: calendar
input huge: int

version v
  cite plan
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  d = 2026-06-01 "a day"
  huge = 100000000000000000000 "10^20"

version v26
  cite plan
  governs 2026
  known 2026-01-01
  cr = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) "s"
  cq = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) "s"
  cn = calendar(weekends [mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat, sun]) "none"

version v28
  cite plan
  governs 2028..
  known 2026-01-01
  cr = calendar(weekends [mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat, sun]) "none"

version v25
  cite plan
  governs ..2025
  known 2026-01-01
  cq = calendar(weekends [mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat, sun]) "none"

ask g_none: add_business(d, huge, cn)
ask g_back: add_business(d, -huge, cq)
ask g_hole: add_business(d, huge, cr)
=== expect
g_back: refused: RUN-DOMAIN calendar cq has no business day
  cut 2026-12-31
g_hole: refused: RUN-LIMIT a day passes 9999-12-31
  cut 2026-12-31
g_none: refused: RUN-DOMAIN calendar cn has no business day
  cut 2026-12-31
